        "math_academy_id": "q-149677",
        "problem": "Find the value of the double sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{12}({a}_{i}-1)({b}_{j}-1)\n$$\n\ngiven that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{a}_{i}=15,\\,{\\sum }_{j=1}^{12}{b}_{j}=16.\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "The product rule for double summations states that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{i}{b}_{j}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{a}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{b}_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the product rule for double summations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{12}({a}_{i}-1)({b}_{j}-1) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}({a}_{i}-1){\\sum }_{j=1}^{12}({b}_{j}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the rules for single summations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}({a}_{i}-1){\\sum }_{j=1}^{12}({b}_{j}-1) & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{a}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}1)\\cdot ({\\sum }_{j=1}^{12}{b}_{j}-{\\sum }_{j=1}^{12}1) \\\\  & =(15-12)\\cdot (16-12) \\\\  & =(3)\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
